DTEND:20261113T223000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Ricardo Jose Ramos Fontes\, University of Califo
 rnia\, Santa Barbara - “Droughts\, Dirty Energy\, and Health”
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Ricardo Jose Ramos Fontes\, University of California
 \, Santa Barbara\nTitle: “Droughts\, Dirty Energy\, and Health”\n\nH
 ydropower-dependent electricity systems are increasingly exposed to drou
 ght risk. This paper studies one consequence of that exposure in Brazil:
  when droughts reduce the availability of hydropower\, the system operat
 ors increase electricity generation from fossil fuels\, and the resultin
 g air pollution exposure raises urgent respiratory morbidity. We combine
  data on plant-level generation\, hydrological conditions\, and ERA5 win
 d fields with hospitalization records from the public health system SUS.
  We first show that droughts reduce hydro inflows and reservoir storage 
 and that lower hydropower generation increases generation from coal\, oi
 l\, and natural gas. We construct a drought-predicted transported fossil
  generation exposure measure by combining predicted thermal dispatch wit
 h source-receptor weights based on distance and wind alignment. We then 
 estimate how increases in power generation from fossil fuels affect urge
 nt hospitalizations in nearby areas. We find that exposure to drought-in
 duced power generation from fossil fuels is associated with higher urgen
 t respiratory morbidity. Perhaps surprisingly\, we find that even power 
 generation from natural gas is associated with large and significant inc
 reases in hospitalization rates due to respiratory conditions. Finally\,
  after the expansion of wind and solar generation in the previous decade
 \, the fossil dispatch response to hydropower scarcity became weaker\; a
  counterfactual analysis suggests that this weaker response reduced drou
 ght-induced ramp-up of generation from fossil fuels and may have averted
  several hundred urgent respiratory admissions. Keywords: Drought\, Hydr
 opower\, Electricity generation\, Fossil fuels\, Respiratory health\, Br
 azil. JEL Classification Codes: I15\, O13\, Q41\, Q42\, Q53 Event Detail
 s Ricardo will be presenting his Advancement to Candidacy paper\, “Dro
 ughts\, Dirty Energy\, and Health”. To access the Advancement paper\, 
 you must have an active UCSB NetID and password.\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.ed
 u/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentation-droughts-dirty-ener
 gy-and-health-ricardo-jose
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-droughts-dirty-energy-and-health-ricardo-jose
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e3991ffe7feda125dbbf@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261029T184823Z
DTSTART:20261117T180000Z
DTEND:20261117T193000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Ryo Aono\, University of California Santa Barbar
 a - “Underprovision by Local Governments During Aging”
LOCATION:North Hall 2113
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Ryo Aono\, University of California Santa Barbara\nT
 itle: “Underprovision by Local Governments During Aging”\n\nLocal go
 vernments in developed countries increasingly struggle to maintain publi
 c goods provision as populations age. Using government finance data for 
 22 OECD countries from 1995 to 2019\, I find that\, as the dependency ra
 tio rises\, local governments tend to cut both investment in stock publi
 c goods and expenditure on flow public goods\, while central governments
  tend to cut investment slightly and increase flow public goods as well 
 as social benefits. To explain these patterns\, I build a calibrated OLG
  model in which central and local governments play a time-consistent Sta
 ckelberg game over a shared tax base\, under rigid rules for pensions an
 d grants. Rising pension obligations raise central taxes\, which\, throu
 gh a vertical tax externality and smaller grants\, constrain local reven
 ue. With more efficient central tax collection and higher substitutabili
 ty across tiers for flow than for stock public goods\, the model reprodu
 ces the observed responses: public goods provision becomes more centrali
 zed\, and composite provision declines. Moderately lowering the pension 
 replacement rate and the grant rate improves welfare by stimulating hous
 ehold savings and improving the efficiency of public resource allocation
 . JEL Classification Codes: H54\, H55\, H72\, H77\, J11\n\nhttps://econ.
 ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentation-underprovisi
 on-local-governments-during-aging
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-underprovision-local-governments-during-aging
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
